{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190581": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Sociology",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY The Sociology Department at Hobart and William Smith Colleges invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning July 1, 2027. Teaching responsibilities in a typical academic year include Introduction to Sociology, electives in the candidate’s area of expertise, and one of the core Sociology courses within the Sociology Program (either classical theory, research methods, statistics, or the senior capstone). In alternate years, the successful candidate will teach a core course in the Public Health program and occasionally a First-Year Seminar, in support of the Colleges’ general curriculum. The standard teaching load is 5 courses a year (a 2/3 load) along with student advising and department and institutional service. We seek candidates with a sociological approach to Public Health and candidates with expertise in classical sociological theory or quantitative methods are especially encouraged to apply. The Sociology department at Hobart and William Smith College is a change-oriented department with expertise in: Criminology, Education, Family, Gender, Race, Social Policy, Urban Life, and Victimology. Applicants should have a record of effective undergraduate teaching that emphasizes critical thinking and active learning. Candidates who incorporate community engagement into their courses are encouraged to describe their plans for continuing this work in the local Geneva, NY community. Candidates should demonstrate a promising research agenda. Areas of research interest include but are not limited to: health policy, healthcare systems, medical sociology, the social determinants of health, epidemiology, aging, occupational health, traditional health practices, and environmental public health. We encourage candidates to discuss whether/how they envision involving undergraduate students in their scholarly agenda. Scholarship at HWS is evaluated relative to a teacher-scholar model rooted in the mission of liberal arts education. The salary range for this position is $60,343 - $73,605. About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. Candidates with a Ph.D. by the time of appointment are preferred but ABD will be considered. While this position is housed within the Sociology Department and we prefer a Sociology Ph.D., candidates from sociology-adjacent fields that center a sociological approach are also encouraged to apply. To learn more about the department, please visit https://www.hws.edu/academics/sociology/default.aspx . For information about the Public Health Program, please visit https://www.hws.edu/academics/public-health/default.aspx . To ensure full consideration please submit via Interfolio: a letter of interest describing qualifications, CV, sample course syllabi, a statement of teaching philosophy with evidence of teaching effectiveness and attention to inclusivity in the classroom, a research statement, scholarly writing sample, and contact information for three individuals to provide letters of reference. We will begin reviewing applications on October 7 and continue until the position is filled.  For fullest consideration, applications should be in prior to the review date. Please contact Kendra Freeman, Search Committee Chair, kefreeman@hws.edu with further inquiries. Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu ). HWS is committed to providing an inclusive and accessible hiring process. If you require disability-related accommodations during the application or interview process, please contact us at hr@hws.edu or 315-781-3312 so we can work with you to meet your needs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189595": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY Hobart and William Smith Colleges invite applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Biology position in Cell Biology, specializing in eukaryotic systems, to begin July 1, 2027. We welcome candidates whose work intersects genetics, molecular biology, and other aspects of eukaryotic cell function. This is a full-time position with a teaching load of five courses distributed across two semesters. The successful candidate will be committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching and will develop an active, undergraduate-focused research program. Teaching responsibilities will include Genetics with lab and an upper-level Cell Biology course with lab. The candidate will have the opportunity to make other contributions to the biology curriculum as well as the general undergraduate curriculum. The five-course teaching load typically consists of two lecture courses with associated labs and one lecture-only course. The new faculty member will be provided with start-up funds to establish their research program and have office and research lab space in the newly constructed Fish Center for the Sciences . The Fish Center will contain modern teaching and research facilities, including cell culture and microscopy facilities, a vivarium, and a greenhouse. In addition to research space, the Colleges have a robust summer science program that provides support for student research opportunities in faculty-led projects. The salary range for this position is $69,360 - $73,605 About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. By the time of appointment, candidates must have a Ph.D. in Cell Biology or a closely related field. Teaching experience and postdoctoral research experience are preferred. Candidates should submit the following materials via Interfolio: 1) cover letter that describes their interest in the position and identifies existing departmental courses they are prepared to teach; new courses they could develop are also welcomed, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) contact information for three confidential professional references, 4) a statement of teaching philosophy that addresses teaching effectiveness and inclusive teaching practices (limit 2 pages), and 5) a statement of research interests (limit 2 pages). Complete applications must be received by September 15, 2026 for full consideration. Specific inquiries about the position may be directed to Dr. Shannon Straub, Chair of the Biology Department ( straub@hws.edu ). Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/197363": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Economics",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY Posted: Nov 10, 2026 A1         General Economics M4         Accounting and Auditing G1         General Financial Markets C10 Econometric and Statistics Methods and Methodology E1          General Aggregative Models M2         Business Economics The Economics Department at Hobart and William Smith Colleges invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning July 1, 2027. Candidates with a Ph.D. by the time of appointment are preferred but ABD will be considered. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate-level courses in accounting, statistics or econometrics, and electives related to candidates’ specialized field, as well as contributing to the general education curriculum. Ability to offer electives in financial economics or other applied field is a plus. Teaching responsibilities include five total courses made up of two sections of accounting; one or two sections of statistics or econometrics; and one or two elective courses related to the candidate’s area of specialization. The successful candidate will be committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching and will develop an active, undergraduate-focused research program. About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. Candidates should upload a letter of application describing teaching qualifications and research interests, c.v., a statement of teaching philosophy with evidence of teaching effectiveness and attention to inclusivity in the classroom, and evidence of successful teaching experience via Interfolio. Applications must be received by December 15, 2026 to be guaranteed full consideration but will be accepted until the position is filled. First round interviews will be conducted virtually in January 2027. Candidates selected for first round interviews will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation and representative sample of research. The salary range for this position is $60,343 - $73,605. Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu ). HWS is committed to providing an inclusive and accessible hiring process. If you require disability-related accommodations during the application or interview process, please contact us at hr@hws.edu or 315-781-3312 so we can work with you to meet your needs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/197460": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship or Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY Posted: Nov 11, 2026 The Department of Asian Studies at Hobart and William Smith Colleges invites applications for a full-time visiting position in the languages and cultures of Southwest Asia and North Africa (SWANA) beginning August 1, 2027. This is a full-time one-year position with a teaching load of either four courses (Postdoctoral Fellowship) or five courses (Visiting Assistant Professor) per year. The appointment has the possibility for renewal contingent on successful annual review and ongoing curricular needs. The applicant will be expected to teach at least three courses per year in Modern Standard Arabic at the elementary and intermediate levels. Additional courses in Arabic literature and/or cinema or associated course(s) will be taught in English. About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. The successful candidate will be able to teach Modern Standard Arabic to beginners and to heritage speakers at the elementary and intermediate level and will be able to support students as they prepare for and return from study abroad in programs in Tunisia and Jordan. The candidate should be prepared to offer a broadly accessible English-language offering that can be cross-listed in Asian Studies, Africana Studies, French and Francophone Studies, Global Studies, or International Relations. Candidates for this position should have a Ph.D. or ABD in Near Eastern Languages and Literature, History, or a related field by summer 2027. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a record of or potential for excellence in undergraduate teaching and the ability to create an inclusive learning environment. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and teaching statement with attention to inclusivity in the classroom via Interfolio. Contact information for three confidential professional references should also be provided. Review of applications will begin immediately, and all applications received by November 30 will be given full consideration. The salary range for this position is $40,228 - $57,800. Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu ). HWS is committed to providing an inclusive and accessible hiring process. If you require disability-related accommodations during the application or interview process, please contact us at hr@hws.edu or 315-781-3312 so we can work with you to meet your needs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/197735": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Gender, Sexuality, and Intersectional Justice",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY Posted: Nov 17, 2026 The Gender, Sexuality, and Intersectional Justice (GSIJ) Department at Hobart and William Smith Colleges invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position with specialization in LGBTQ+ Studies beginning July 1, 2027. We especially encourage candidates with subfield expertise in transnational, diasporic, and/or indigenous genders, sexualities, and social movements, and who demonstrate commitment to interdisciplinarity and collective inquiry. The standard teaching load is 5 courses a year (2/3 load) along with student advising and department and institutional service. Teaching responsibilities in a typical academic year include Intro to GSIJ, q ueer/trans theory and methods, electives at the 200- and 300-level in area of expertise across the LGBTQ+ Studies major, and occasionally a First-Year Seminar, in support of the Colleges’ general curriculum . To learn more about the majors and minors within the department, please visit https://www.hws.edu/academics/gender-sexuality-and-intersectional-justice/default.aspx . Candidates should demonstrate a promising research agenda. Areas of research interest could include but are not limited to: transnational, diasporic and/or indigenous genders and sexualities, transgender studies, disability and crip studies, critical museum or curatorial studies, Africana studies, Asian studies, indigenous studies, abolition feminisms and carceral studies, critical legal studies, book arts and material culture studies. We encourage candidates to discuss how they envision involving undergraduate students in their scholarly agenda. About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. Candidates with a Ph.D. or terminal degree in their field by the time of appointment are preferred but ABD will be considered. While this position is housed within the GSIJ Department, we encourage candidates from any interdisciplinary fields that center queer and feminist approaches to apply. Candidates from disciplinary fields (including but not limited to English, History, Anthropology, etc) are also welcome to apply but will need to demonstrate interdisciplinary teaching and scholarship to be successful. To ensure full consideration, please submit via Interfolio: CV, a letter of interest, 1-2 page teaching statement that demonstrates how feminist social-justice oriented pedagogy informs your teaching and course design, a sample of your scholarship such as a journal article or dissertation chapter, and contact information for three individuals to provide letters of reference. Letters of reference will be solicited from candidates who proceed to the interview stage of the search process. We will begin reviewing applications on January 15 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Michelle Martin-Baron, Search Committee Chair, mmartinbaron@hws.edu with further inquiries . The salary range for this position is $60,343 - $73,605. Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu ). HWS is committed to providing an inclusive and accessible hiring process. If you require disability-related accommodations during the application or interview process, please contact us at hr@hws.edu or 315-781-3312 so we can work with you to meet your needs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/198058": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Politics",
      "description": "Department: Hobart William Smith Colleges Location: Geneva, NY Posted: Nov 23, 2026 The Department of Politics of Hobart and William Smith Colleges invites applications for a Visiting Assistant Professor of Politics position beginning July 1, 2027. This is a full-time, one-year position with a teaching load of five or six courses over two semesters. The appointment has the possibility for renewal contingent on successful annual review and ongoing curricular needs. We seek a scholar who can teach introductory and upper-level courses on power, political and social movements, and political thought in America. We welcome candidates whose teaching and research examine how individuals and groups organize to influence government, mobilize to contest or defend existing institutions, and engage in struggles over democracy in the United States. Areas of interest might include environmental politics and movements; conservative and populist movements; religion and politics; immigration; anti-war movements; minority political mobilization; and rural, urban, and labor politics. Other areas of expertise that complement the position’s focus on power, political participation, and social movements are also welcome. Research and teaching in the department are not confined by conventional subfields. We welcome a broad range of theoretical, historical, and critical approaches to power and political life, including interdisciplinarity in scholarship. We also value teaching that encourages students to engage thoughtfully with competing perspectives and interpretations. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. or be ABD in Political Science, or a closely related field, at the time of appointment. About Hobart and William Smith Hobart and William Smith is a nationally recognized liberal arts and sciences college in New York’s Finger Lakes region, enrolling approximately 2,000 students and maintaining a 13:1 student-faculty ratio. Known for its relationship-rich educational experience and commitment to student success, HWS combines outstanding teaching, engaged scholarship and meaningful mentorship to prepare graduates for fulfilling careers and lives. One of the few undergraduate institutions designated a Carnegie Top Research institution, HWS offers faculty exceptional opportunities to pursue ambitious scholarship while collaborating closely with undergraduates. Faculty benefit from strong support for research and creative activity, interdisciplinary collaboration, community-engaged learning, and a vibrant academic community dedicated to innovation, intellectual inquiry and student outcomes. Please submit a letter of application, a curriculum vitae, a teaching statement with evidence of teaching effectiveness and attention to inclusivity in the classroom , a sample syllabus, and a writing sample via Interfolio. Applicants should also provide contact information for three professional references. References will be contacted only for candidates who advance in the search. Applications received by January 20, 2027, will receive first consideration, but we will continue to review applications until the position is filled. Additional inquiries may be directed to Professor DeWayne Lucas, Search Chair, at lucas@hws.edu . Salary range for this position is $50,286 - $69,360. Questions regarding Interfolio or visa status should be directed to Human Resources ( hr@hws.edu ). HWS is committed to providing an inclusive and accessible hiring process. If you require disability-related accommodations during the application or interview process, please contact us at hr@hws.edu or 315-781-3312 so we can work with you to meet your needs."
    }
